-- stock_reservations, payments, payment_notifications, customer_addresses, shipping_areas
-- (empty), order_access_tokens, order_notifications, and checkout columns on orders. Generated by
-- drizzle-kit, then read; the lock_timeout line and the set_updated_at triggers at the end are
-- hand-added. No reference rows: nothing the code matches by literal key is introduced.
--
-- Backward compatibility with the previous release (CLAUDE.md expand/contract): additive only.
-- No DROP, rename or type change. New tables are unread by the previous release. Every new orders
-- column is nullable, or (needs_attention) NOT NULL with a constant default, which Postgres 11+
-- adds without a table rewrite; the previous release's INSERT/UPDATE statements name only the
-- columns they know and so never touch them. Every new orders CHECK holds for those writes:
-- payment_status stays NULL (so the web-only, checkout-fields and paid_at checks pass),
-- cancel_reason stays NULL, needs_attention stays '{}'. The new UNIQUE (id, order_id, sku) on
-- order_items is implied by its primary key, so it can never reject a write. Rollback leaves the
-- tables and columns in place, unread by the older image.
--
-- Locks: the orders/order_items ALTERs take ACCESS EXCLUSIVE, and the new FKs take SHARE ROW
-- EXCLUSIVE on orders and customers; all are held until the migrator's single transaction
-- commits. The CHECKs and the UNIQUE index scan tables of a few thousand rows at most, so that is
-- well under a second. lock_timeout bounds the wait to acquire each lock: the migration fails
-- fast (and rolls back entirely, so a re-run starts clean) instead of queueing every order read
-- behind a long-running transaction. SET LOCAL lasts until that transaction ends.
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '5s';--> statement-breakpoint
